# ffbox secrets. `sh ffbox/setup.sh` installs this template at ~/.config/ffbox/secrets.env with
# mode 600 and never overwrites it afterwards; fill in the empty values below and you are done.
#
# This file stays on the host. Docker reads it at run time and injects the values as environment
# variables; nothing here is ever baked into the image (image layers are readable by anyone who
# can run `docker history`, and a baked secret survives in the layer cache forever).
#
# Do NOT pass these as command-line arguments instead — argv is world-readable through
# /proc/<pid>/cmdline for the life of the process, and it lands in your shell history.

# --- Claude ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Long-lived subscription token. Generate on the host with:
#   claude setup-token
# Bills against your Claude subscription, no API key involved, and unlike mounting
# ~/.claude/.credentials.json it can't be rewritten underneath you by a refresh.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# --- Unity (HOST SIDE ONLY) ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are read by the HOST, to mint and refresh the licence file. They are NEVER passed into a
# container -- not an agent run, not a pool worker, not a CI job. That distinction is the whole of
# this change, and it is the same posture GH_PR_TOKEN and the Discord token below already have.
#
# WHY THEY ARE STILL HERE. Until 2026-09-01 ffbox handed these to every container, which activated
# online on start. That put a full Unity account credential -- the same identity as the Asset Store
# account and the org membership -- inside a container running
# `claude -p --dangerously-skip-permissions` over text strangers wrote, readable out of
# /proc/self/environ by anything in it.
#
# The container now gets a .ulf FILE instead, mounted read-only. But a Personal .ulf carries a
# rolling ~24-hour UpdateDate rather than an annual expiry (measured: no StopDate at all), so
# something has to renew it -- and that something is the host, right before it creates a container:
#
#     ffbox/unity-offline-license.sh ensure 4
#
# runs at every container launch, does nothing but read a date when the licence is healthy, and
# re-activates when under four hours remain. Leave these blank and the licence cannot renew itself;
# `mint` will prompt for them interactively instead.
#
# AN ACCESS TOKEN IS BETTER THAN THE PASSWORD where you can get one: it expires, it is revocable
# without a password reset, and it is the only way past 2FA on the account.
#UNITY_ACCESS_TOKEN=
UNITY_EMAIL=
UNITY_PASSWORD=

# NOT NEEDED, AND NOT USED. UNITY_SERIAL and UNITY_LICENSE_FILE belonged to the old online serial
# activation; nothing reads them now. A Personal licence has no serial to give.

# --- ffweb (the page) ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# ffweb serves over https behind a login. The accounts are hardcoded in ffweb.py — Ben and
# Lothsahn, sharing a passphrase — and these override without a patch, which is the point of
# them being here: a machine on a LAN address should not run the password that is in git.
#
# FFWEB_PASSWORD sets the password for every account. FFWEB_USER narrows the table to that one
# name (matched case-insensitively) with that password, and drops the rest.
#FFWEB_USER=
#FFWEB_PASSWORD=
#
# The certificate is self-signed and generated into ~/ffbox-state/tls on first start, so the
# browser warns once and that warning is correct. Point --tls-cert/--tls-key at a real pair if
# you have one; ffweb never overwrites a certificate it finds.

# --- Discord + GitHub (HOST SIDE ONLY) -----------------------------------------------------
# Neither of these is ever passed into a container, and that is load-bearing rather than
# tidiness.
#
# FFDISCORD_APP_TOKEN is the bot token (FFDISCORD_TOKEN is the older name and still works).
# The host does every Discord read and every Discord write:
# ffwatch pulls messages and attachments before a run and posts the reply after it, so the
# agent — which is reading text written by strangers — never holds a credential that can speak
# as the bot. ffdiscord and ffdiscord-listener both read this, or the same value from the
# "discord.app_token" key in ~/.config/ffbox/config.json.
#FFDISCORD_APP_TOKEN=
#
# GH_PR_TOKEN is what opens pull requests, and opening them is ALL it may do. "Nothing merges,
# ever" is held by this token never entering the container, NOT by the agent's deny list — a deny
# pattern like Bash(git push*) is a tripwire that `sh -c 'git push'` walks straight through. The
# container has no push credential and no gh binary, so publication is physically the host's job.
#
# A FINE-GRAINED token: resource owner Final-Factory, repository access Final-Factory/FinalFactory
# and nothing else, PULL REQUESTS at read and write, every other permission at No access. Metadata
# read is added for you. Do NOT give it contents:write, whatever an older copy of this file said:
# merging a pull request needs contents:write alongside pull-requests:write, so a token carrying
# both is a token that can merge, and this one is deliberately not. Pushing the work branch uses a
# SEPARATE credential -- the one git finds in ~/.git-credentials -- which is never spliced into a
# push url, because argv is world-readable through /proc. ffbox/CREDENTIALS.md has the whole split.
#
# ffwatch reads it from the ENVIRONMENT rather than from ~/.config/ffbox/config.json, so it never
# sits on disk beside channel ids. GH_TOKEN is still read, as the name this had before 2026-09-02.
#GH_PR_TOKEN=
